From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Philippov <paul@themactep.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 13:45:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] MIPS: xburst: t10: export the gpio-keys platform data symbols

The gpio-userkeys module (built from the Ingenic SDK tree) has no board
headers of its own; it takes the board's button table, platform data, and
platform device by name:

    extern struct platform_device jz_button_device;
    extern struct gpio_keys_button board_buttons[];
    extern struct gpio_keys_platform_data board_button_data;

Every supported SoC exports those three symbols from its isvp
keyboard_gpio.c, which is what lets the module link.  T10 never did, so
on T10 the module fails to load:

    modprobe: can't load module gpio-userkeys (ingenic/gpio-userkeys.ko):
    unknown symbol in module, or unknown parameter

and the user button is dead.  Export the three symbols like T20, T21,
T23, T30, and T31 do.

Signed-off-by: Paul Philippov <paul@themactep.com>
---
 arch/mips/xburst/soc-t10/chip-t10/isvp/common/keyboard_gpio.c | 4 ++++
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/mips/xburst/soc-t10/chip-t10/isvp/common/keyboard_gpio.c b/arch/mips/xburst/soc-t10/chip-t10/isvp/common/keyboard_gpio.c
--- a/arch/mips/xburst/soc-t10/chip-t10/isvp/common/keyboard_gpio.c
+++ b/arch/mips/xburst/soc-t10/chip-t10/isvp/common/keyboard_gpio.c
@@ -140,3 +140,7 @@
                 .platform_data	= &board_button_data,
 	}
 };
+
+EXPORT_SYMBOL(board_buttons);
+EXPORT_SYMBOL(board_button_data);
+EXPORT_SYMBOL(jz_button_device);
